Ore-Grit

Creative Catalyst: Bored miners two kilometers beneath the crust transformed rhythmic percussive pipe-banging into a cavernous, sub-harmonic ritual.

This music sounds like the pressure of a mountain distilled into sound; it is dense, metallic, and claustrophobic. Instead of airy, wide-stage atmospherics, it utilizes a "compressed-cavern" acoustic—everything feels like it is vibrating inside a hollowed-out skull. The Neurofunk basslines aren't just low-end; they are synthesized imitations of shifting tectonic plates, while the 90s Coldwave influence manifests as brittle, high-frequency synth stabs that cut through the bass like a carbide drill. It is psychedelic, not by drifting into space, but by digging so deep into the material world that the physics of sound begin to warp and hallucinate.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 135–142 BPM; a jagged, limping gait that mirrors the rhythm of heavy-duty hydraulic machinery.
*   Harmonic Palette: Dissonant Locrian modes, metallic microtonal drones, and "rusty" synth leads that favor biting harmonic overtones over melody.
*   Production Signatures: 
       Pipe-Gating:* Using physical field recordings of ventilation-shaft resonances as side-chain gates for the entire rhythm section.
       Depth-Compression:* An extreme, claustrophobic mastering technique where all high frequencies are crushed by low-end energy, mimicking the feeling of intense geological pressure.
       Ferrous Delay:* A custom tape-saturation circuit that introduces randomized metallic clatter into the repeats.
*   Dynamics: Tracks start with isolated percussive strikes, building into monolithic, suffocating walls of sound before abruptly collapsing into silence.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the copper-rich depths of the Chilean Andes, Ore-Grit is the music of the "Sub-Terrestrials." These miners, isolated for months at a time, began treating their work-site machinery as a symphony. Their fashion—grease-stained canvas jumpsuits, reinforced brass-goggles to shield against pipe-bursts, and reflective thermal-mining tape stitched into jagged patterns—became the standard aesthetic. Rituals involve "The Descent," a silent march into the lower galleries to play sets on the pipes during seismic shifts, creating a symbiotic vibration between the music and the earth itself.

Instruments: Custom-welded steel pipe percussion arrays, Korg MS-20 (for biting, abrasive leads), modified 90s hardware samplers (Akai S1000s loaded with grit-samples), and analog Neuro-bass oscillators.

For Fans Of: Lorn, Throbbing Gristle, early Venetian Snares, and Second-wave Coldwave.

